      "In the new-hire prerequisite latency protocol, stage 3 records that primary method: follow each prerequisite from valid request through source receipt, owner decision, execution, and independently verified readiness. Freeze the protocol before reviewing results. Use pseudonymous case keys and preserve the source versions available at the time of action. Keep source fact, administrative preparation, owner decision, execution, communication, and verified destination state in separate fields rather than reconstructing them from one status label.",
      "In the new-hire prerequisite latency protocol, stage 4 records that define the unit of analysis before sampling. One employee, request, field, prerequisite, or destination can produce different denominators and conclusions. Record linked events without counting the same underlying case as several independent successes. When a case crosses periods, state whether it remains in its original cohort or enters a clearly labeled carryover analysis.",
      "In the new-hire prerequisite latency protocol, stage 5 records that create an analysis plan for time to evidence-ready state, unresolved-age bands, rework, incorrect closure, and readiness reversals. Report counts beside rates, medians beside ranges, and age bands beside averages. Show every predefined high-risk exception and a random sample of ordinary cases. An aggregate score must not allow many easy records to offset one unauthorized employment, pay, privacy, benefits, access, or security action.",
      "In the new-hire prerequisite latency protocol, stage 6 records that evidence collection should use a dated source inventory. For each variable, name its authoritative system, permitted extraction, transformation, reviewer, and retention rule. Hash or version exported extracts when practical. If a source changes after observation, preserve both versions and identify which one informed the contemporaneous action.",
      "In the new-hire prerequisite latency protocol, stage 7 records that reviewer independence matters. Train reviewers on definitions with cases outside the study sample, then record their first judgments before discussion. Compare disagreements about facts separately from disagreements about authority or severity. Adjudication should create an audit trail; it must not erase the initial result merely because an owner later supplied missing information.",
      "In the new-hire prerequisite latency protocol, stage 8 records that the adverse-case test removes one expected source, delays one acknowledgment, and introduces one plausible conflicting record. Reviewers should mark unknown, restrict access, and route a precise question rather than choosing a convenient answer. Repeat with a normal case to ensure the process does not over-escalate routine work or collect unnecessary employee data.",
      "In the new-hire prerequisite latency protocol, stage 9 records that control for late hiring changes, differing role complexity, unavailable vendors, and system timestamps that record entry rather than occurrence. Predefine how each factor will be represented, stratified, or reported as a limitation. Do not imply causation from a before-and-after comparison when staffing mix, systems, policy, owner availability, or case difficulty also changed. Sensitivity analysis should show which conclusions survive reasonable alternate cutoffs and missing-evidence treatments.",
      "In the new-hire prerequisite latency protocol, stage 10 records that privacy and security controls should follow purpose limitation, named accounts, multifactor authentication, least privilege, controlled exports, and logged access. Store the crosswalk between case keys and identities only where authorized. The analytical file should contain the minimum fields needed to answer the protocol and should expire under a documented retention decision.",
      "In the new-hire prerequisite latency protocol, stage 11 records that a quality review checks source completeness, timestamp meaning, duplicate cases, impossible sequences, missing destinations, and values changed after the observation. Investigate anomalies rather than silently normalizing them. A blank value, an unavailable record, and a confirmed negative state are analytically different and should never share one code.",
      "In the new-hire prerequisite latency protocol, stage 12 records that interpretation should distinguish process evidence from employee or business outcomes. A complete packet does not prove the underlying decision was correct; a fast acknowledgment does not prove the destination changed; reviewer agreement does not prove the rule was sound. Conclusions should stay within the tested population, period, systems, and definitions.",
      "In the new-hire prerequisite latency protocol, stage 13 records that governance requires a named protocol owner, data custodian, operational owner, privacy or security reviewer when relevant, and decision maker for remediation. Coordinators may prepare evidence and run documented checks. Qualified owners retain legal interpretation, employment status, pay, eligibility, policy, sensitive disclosure, access, and acceptance of residual risk.",
      "In the new-hire prerequisite latency protocol, stage 14 records that the result table should show the numerator, denominator, unresolved count, exclusions with reasons, observation coverage, and confidence or uncertainty appropriate to the design. Include a case-flow diagram and a discrepancy appendix without identifiable employee narratives. Record whether missing evidence is concentrated by system, owner, case class, or time period.",
      "In the new-hire prerequisite latency protocol, stage 15 records that corrective actions need a condition, evidence reference, accountable owner, due date, implementation record, verification method, and residual limitation. Training can address misunderstanding; it cannot repair a missing integration, contradictory policy, excessive permission model, or absent decision owner. Resample after the change using fresh cases and the frozen definition.",
      "In the new-hire prerequisite latency protocol, stage 16 records that stopping rules should pause the study or operational pilot for unauthorized disclosure, unsafe access, action without required approval, material source corruption, or an inability to preserve the promised confidentiality boundary. Record the stop, protect affected evidence, notify the authorized response owner, and resume only under a documented decision.",
